course: medicine
lesson: 21
title: What vitamins do
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Vitamins are substances the body needs in tiny amounts to work properly, and because it can't make enough of most of them, they have to come from food.
explain: Minerals like calcium and iron are needed too: calcium builds bones and teeth, and iron helps make red blood cells that carry oxygen.
explain: Long ago sailors on months-long voyages got scurvy, with bleeding gums and wounds that would not heal, until they took lemons and limes: the missing piece was vitamin C.
explain: A plate with many colours, fruit, vegetables, wholegrains, dairy or other calcium foods, and protein, gives almost everything a healthy child needs without any special drinks.
fact: Vitamins are needed in small amounts, and most must come from food because the body can't make enough of them.
fact: Carrots contain beta-carotene, which the body turns into vitamin A, needed for seeing in dim light; extra carrots do not give night vision.
fact: The story that carrots let British pilots see in the dark was spread in the Second World War, partly to hide the use of radar, machines that found enemy planes with radio waves.
fact: Vitamin C helps wounds heal and keeps gums and skin healthy, and sailors once got scurvy from lacking it.
fact: Calcium builds bones and teeth, and vitamin D helps the body absorb it; milk, cheese, yoghurt, green vegetables, beans, tofu and fortified foods (with extra vitamins or minerals added) all contain calcium.
fact: Iron is needed to make red blood cells, and too little causes anaemia, which makes people tired and pale; a blood test can check iron levels.
fact: Spinach contains iron, but the body absorbs it poorly, so spinach is not the iron superfood of cartoons.
fact: B vitamins help the body turn food into energy, and vegans usually need a B12 supplement, an extra pill, because B12 comes mainly from animal foods.
fact: Some vitamins, such as A and D, are stored in the body, and taking too much over time can be harmful.
fact: Vegetarians can get all their nutrients from beans, eggs, dairy, nuts, grains and vegetables.
fact: Fibre is the part of plants the body can't digest, and it helps food move through the gut and keeps you full.
fact: In the UK a small glass of fruit juice (150 ml) counts as one of your five a day, but only once a day, however much you drink, because juice is sugary.
exercise: What are vitamins? | Substances the body needs in small amounts, mostly from food | Big meals that give energy | Germs that live in the gut | Medicines only doctors give
exercise: Will eating lots of carrots let you see in the dark? | No, they help normal eyes but give no night vision | Yes, after a week of carrots | Yes, but only orange carrots | No, carrots are bad for eyes
exercise: Why was the carrot story spread in the Second World War? | Partly to hide the use of radar | To sell more carrots to France | Because pilots ate only carrots | Because scientists had just proved it
exercise: What disease did sailors get without vitamin C? | Scurvy | Rickets | Chickenpox | Asthma
exercise: What does vitamin C help with? | Healing wounds and healthy gums | Growing taller overnight while you sleep | Seeing clearly in the dark at night | Making your bones soft and bendy
exercise: What do calcium and vitamin D do together? | Build strong bones and teeth | Make all of the red blood cells | Turn every bit of food into energy | Fight off colds and flu for you
exercise: Which food besides milk has calcium? | Tofu and green vegetables | Only white bread and toast | Fizzy drinks like cola and lemonade | Crisps and other salty snacks
exercise: Why might Mum take iron tablets? | Low iron caused anaemia and made her tired | Iron makes hair curly | Iron cures colds | Iron keeps her teeth white and her nails strong
exercise: Is spinach a miracle iron food? | No, its iron is poorly absorbed | Yes, it makes muscles grow at once | Yes, it has more iron than anything | No, it has no iron at all
exercise: What do B vitamins help the body do? | Turn food into energy | Make vitamin D from sunlight | Grow hair faster | Block germs
exercise: Can too many vitamins make you ill? | Yes, especially vitamins A and D taken in excess | No, the more the better | No, the body throws all extra away | Only vitamin C
exercise: Can a vegetarian be healthy? | Yes, with beans, eggs, dairy, nuts and grains | No, they always lack protein | Only if they take ten different pills every day | Only adults can
exercise: What does fibre do? | Helps food move through the gut | Makes your bones hard and strong | Gives you good night vision in the dark | Kills the bacteria in your tummy
exercise: How many of your five a day can fruit juice count for? | One at most, however much you drink | All of them, as many glasses as you drink | None at all | Three if it's fresh
exercise: Who usually needs a B12 supplement? | Vegans | Everyone who eats meat | Only babies | People who drink milk
test: Grandad: "eat ur carrots, pilots saw in the dark in the war". What really helped them? | Radar, the carrot story was a cover | Carrot juice painted on their goggles | Special night-vision carrots | Eating 20 carrots a day
test: Popeye eats spinach and gets huge muscles. What's the real story? | Spinach is good food but its iron is poorly absorbed and gives no super-strength | Spinach really builds muscles instantly | Spinach has no goodness at all | Only tinned spinach works
test: What would happen to a sailor on a year-long voyage with no fruit or vegetables? | He could get scurvy from lack of vitamin C | He would get stronger bones | Nothing, because ship biscuits have all the vitamins | He would get night vision
test: Jay can't drink milk. Where else can his bones get calcium? | Fortified plant drinks, tofu, beans and greens | Nowhere, he'll have weak bones | Only from tablets | From fizzy drinks
test: "4 glasses of OJ = 4 of my five a day innit" — right? | No, juice counts once, and it's sugary | Yes, every single glass counts as one, innit | Yes, as long as it's the kind with bits in | No, juice counts as zero, it's just a drink
test: Mia says vegetarians all go weak. What's true? | Vegetarians can get all nutrients from a varied diet | Mia is right, they always go weak | Only vegans can be healthy | Vegetarians need daily injections
test: Why are vitamins A and D riskier to overdo than vitamin C? | They are stored in the body and can build up | They are poisonous in any amount, even a tiny one | Vitamin C is not a real vitamin | A and D are only in sweets
test: Which plate gives the widest range of vitamins? | Fish, rice, broccoli, peppers and a yoghurt | Chips, sausages, ketchup and a big fizzy drink | White bread and jam | A big bowl of crisps
test: "Energy drinks have B vitamins so they give u energy" — fair? | No, B vitamins help use food's energy, they aren't extra fuel | Yes, B vitamins are pure energy | Yes, that's why sport stars drink them | No, B vitamins make you sleepy
test: Kid eats white bread, no fruit, no veg, and can't poo for days. What's missing most? | Fibre | Vitamin D | Calcium | Salt
test: You're pale and tired all the time. Which could a blood test check? | Iron levels | How many carrots you ate | Your eye colour | Your height
exam: Identify the principal function of ascorbic acid, hereinafter vitamin C, in the human body. | The healing of wounds and the maintenance of healthy gums | The production of clear vision in conditions of total darkness | The conversion of sunlight into bone | The prevention of all infection
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the consumption of carrots and nocturnal vision. | Normal intake supports ordinary vision but confers no night vision | Abundant intake confers the power of night vision | Carrots impair vision in dim light | Carrots contain no precursor of vitamin A
exam: Whereas a child consumes four glasses of fruit juice daily, determine the number of portions credited towards the recommended five. | One portion only | Four portions | Five portions | No portion whatsoever
exam: Ascertain which statement most accurately characterises the vegetarian diet in childhood. | It can supply all requisite nutrients if suitably varied | It invariably results in protein deficiency | It is incompatible with growth | It necessitates daily injections of vitamins and of protein
